function e(e){return e.filter(e=>!!e.marker).map(e=>{let t=e.text.indexOf(`. `),n=t>0?e.text.slice(0,t+1):e.text,r=n.length>60?`${n.slice(0,60).replace(/[\s,;:]+$/,``)}…`:n;return{key:e.marker,text:r}})}var t={id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-mp`,title:`A South Korean city designed for the future takes on a life of its own`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-mp-A`,marker:`A`,text:`Getting around a city is one thing—and then there's the matter of getting from one city to another. One vision of the perfect city of the future is a place that offers easy access to air travel. In 2011, a University of North Carolina business professor named John Kasarda published a book called Aerotropolis: The Way We'll Live Next. Kasarda says future cities should be built intentionally around or near airports. The idea, as he has put it, is to offer businesses 'rapid, long-distance connectivity on a massive scale.'`},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-mp-B`,marker:`B`,text:`'The 18th century really was a waterborne century, the 19th century a rail century, the 20th century a highway, car, truck century—and the 21st century will increasingly be an aviation century, as the globe becomes increasingly connected by air,' Kasarda says. Songdo, a city built from scratch in South Korea, is one of Kasarda's prime examples. It has existed for just a few years. 'From the outset, it was designed on the basis of connectivity and competitiveness,' says Kasarda. 'The government built the bridge directly from the airport to the Songdo International Business District. And the surface infrastructure was built at the same time as the new airport.'`},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-mp-C`,marker:`C`,text:`Songdo is a stone's throw from South Korea's Incheon Airport, its main international hub. But it takes a lot more than a nearby airport to be a city of the future. Just building a place as an 'international business district' doesn't mean it will become one. Park Yeon Soo conceived this city of the future back in 1986. He considers Songdo his baby. Park sees himself as a visionary. Thirty years after he imagined the city, Park's baby is close to 70 percent built, with 36,000 people living in the business district and 90,000 residents in greater Songdo. It's about an hour outside Seoul, built on former tidal flats along the Yellow Sea. There's a Coast Guard building and a tall trade tower, as well as a park, golf course, and university.`},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-mp-D`,marker:`D`,text:`Chances are you've actually seen this place. Songdo appears in the most famous music video ever to come out of South Korea. 'Gangnam Style' refers to the fashionable Gangnam district in Seoul. But some of the video was filmed in Songdo. 'I don't know if you remember, there was a scene in a subway station. That was not Gangnam. That was actually Songdo,' says Jung Won Son, a professor of urban development at London's Bartlett School of Planning. 'Part of the reason to shoot there is that it's new and nice.'`},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-mp-E`,marker:`E`,text:`The city was supposed to be a hub for global companies, with employees from all over the world. But that's not how it has turned out. Songdo's reputation is as a futuristic ghost town. But the reality is more complicated. A bridge with big, light-blue loops leads into the business district. In the center of the main road, there's a long line of flags of the world. On the corner, there's a Starbucks and a 7-Eleven—all of the international brands that you see all over the world nowadays.`},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-mp-F`,marker:`F`,text:`The city is not empty. There are mothers pushing baby carriages, old women with walkers—even in the middle of the day, when it's 90 degrees out. Byun Young-Jin chairs the Songdo real estate association and started selling property here when the first phase of the city opened in 2005. He says demand has boomed in the past couple of years. Most of his clients are Korean. In fact, the developer says, 99 percent of the homes here are sold to Koreans. Young families move here because the schools are great. And that's the problem: Songdo has become a popular Korean city—more popular as a residential area than a business one. It's not yet the futuristic international business hub that planners imagined. 'It's a great place to live. And it's becoming a great place to work,' says Scott Summers, vice president of Gale International, the developer of the city. The floor-to-ceiling windows of his company's offices overlook Songdo Central Park, with a canal full of small boats and people fishing. Shimmering glass towers line the canal's edge.`},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-mp-G`,marker:`G`,text:`'What's happened is that our focus on creating that quality of life first has enabled the residents to live here,' Summers says. But there needs to be strong economic incentives for companies to locate here. The city is still unfinished, and it feels a bit like a theme park. It doesn't feel all that futuristic. There's a high-tech underground trash disposal system. Buildings are environmentally friendly. Everybody's television set is connected to a system that streams personalized language or exercise classes.`},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-mp-H`,marker:`H`,text:`But this is not Star Trek. And to some of the residents, Songdo feels hollow. 'I'm, like, in prison for weekdays. That's what we call it in the workplace,' says a woman in her 20s. She doesn't want to use her name for fear of being fired from her job. She goes back to Seoul every weekend. 'I say I'm prison-breaking on Friday nights.' But she has to make the prison break in her own car. There's no high-speed train connecting Songdo to Seoul, just over 20 miles away.`},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-mp-I`,marker:`I`,text:`Park Yeon Soo, the man who first imagined Songdo, feels frustrated, too. He says he built South Korea a luxury vehicle, 'like Mercedes or BMW. It's a good car now. But we're waiting for a good driver to accelerate.' But there are lots of other good cars out there, too. The world is dotted with futuristic, high-tech cities trying to attract the biggest international companies.`},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-mp-J`,marker:`J`,text:`Songdo's backers contend that it's still early, and business space is filling up—about 70 percent of finished offices are now occupied. Brent Ryan, who teaches urban design at MIT, says Songdo proves a universal principle. 'There have been a lot of utopian cities in history. And the reason we don't know about a lot of them is that they have vanished entirely.' In other words, when it comes to cities—or anything else—it is hard to predict the future.`}]},n={"cet4-2019-12-s3":{subtitle:`2019 年 12 月 · 第 3 套`,minutes:125,words:1400,tags:[`真题`,`四级`],summary:`2019年12月英语四级第3套真题，写作考给想在华学习的外国朋友写信推荐一所大学（参考范文推荐清华大学），翻译考中国汉族人姓名的构成与特点，阅读涉及松岛未来城市、汽水税与微波炉碳排放，听力给出原文并由站内合成朗读。`,parts:[{id:`writing`,kind:`writing`,title:`Part I 写作`,instructions:`Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write a letter to a foreign friend who wants to study in China. Please recommend a university to him. You should write at least 120 words but no more than 180 words.`,minutes:30,reference:{essay:`Dear John, I'm thrilled to hear that you're considering studying in China. Given your interest in technology and innovation, I highly recommend Tsinghua University in Beijing. As one of China's premier institutions, it boasts a robust engineering program and a vibrant campus life. Firstly, Tsinghua's engineering faculty is renowned for its cutting-edge research and international collaborations. You'll have the opportunity to work with top-notch professors and engage in groundbreaking projects. Moreover, the university's strategic location in Beijing provides easy access to tech companies and startups, offering ample internship and networking opportunities. In addition to its academic strengths, Tsinghua offers a diverse student body and a rich cultural experience. You'll be able to immerse yourself in Chinese language and culture while making friends from all over the world. I believe that studying at Tsinghua will be a life-changing experience for you. I'm confident that you'll thrive there and make the most of your time in China. Looking forward to your decision. Best regards, [Your Name]`,guidance:[`书信格式与开篇点题：Dear John, I'm thrilled to hear that you're considering studying in China. Given your interest in technology and innovation, I highly recommend Tsinghua University in Beijing 以书信称呼开头，回应"给想在华学习的外国朋友写信推荐一所大学"的任务，借对方对科技创新的兴趣自然引出推荐清华大学。`,`推荐理由一（学术实力）：As one of China's premier institutions, it boasts a robust engineering program... Firstly, Tsinghua's engineering faculty is renowned for its cutting-edge research and international collaborations 写清华作为中国顶尖学府、工程学科实力雄厚、研究前沿且国际合作广泛；work with top-notch professors and engage in groundbreaking projects 补充与世界一流教授共事、参与开创性项目的机会。`,`推荐理由二（地理与实践机会）：Moreover, the university's strategic location in Beijing provides easy access to tech companies and startups, offering ample internship and networking opportunities 写地处北京、便于接触科技公司与初创企业、实习与人脉机会充足；In addition to its academic strengths... a diverse student body and a rich cultural experience 补充多元学生群体与丰富文化体验，紧扣"来华学习"主题。`,`结尾总结与祝愿：I believe that studying at Tsinghua will be a life-changing experience for you. I'm confident that you'll thrive there 总结推荐、表达信心；末句 Looking forward to your decision. Best regards, [Your Name] 收束书信格式。全文约 150 词，符合 120–180 词要求。`]},questions:[{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-w1`,number:1,type:`writing`,stem:`For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write a letter to a foreign friend who wants to study in China. Please recommend a university to him. You should write at least 120 words but no more than 180 words.`,answer:`见参考`,explanation:`命题书信：给想在华学习的外国朋友写信推荐一所大学，须说明推荐理由。参考范文推荐清华大学（Tsinghua University），开篇用 Dear John 书信称呼并借用对方对科技创新的兴趣亮明推荐意图；主体从顶尖学府与工程学科实力、前沿研究与国际合作、北京地理位置带来的实习与人脉机会、多元文化与丰富体验四方面论证；结尾总结并以 Best regards 收束。结构为"点题—理由—总结"，词数 120–180。`,score:15}]},{id:`listening`,kind:`listening`,title:`Part II 听力理解`,instructions:`Directions: In this section, you will hear three news reports. At the end of each news report, you will hear two or three questions. Both the news report and the questions will be spoken only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C) and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 1 with a single line through the centre. Directions: In this section, you will hear two long conversations. At the end of each conversation, you will hear four questions. Both the conversation and the questions will be spoken only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C) and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 1 with a single line through the centre. Directions: In this section, you will hear three passages. At the end of each passage, you will hear three or four questions. Both the passage and the questions will be spoken only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C) and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 1 with a single line through the centre.`,minutes:25,audio:{note:`站内合成朗读（非考试原音）`,segments:[{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-ls-1`,label:`News Report 1`,questionNo:1,text:`New York City Police captured a cow on the loose in Prospect Park on Tuesday after the animal became an attraction for tourists while walking along the streets and enjoying the park facilities. The confused creature and camera-holding humans stared at each other through a fence for several minutes. At other times, the cow wandered around the 526-acre park and the artificial grass field normally used for human sporting events. Officers used soccer goals to fence the animal in. However, the cow then moved through one of the nets, knocking down a police officer in the process. Police eventually trapped the cow between two vehicles parked on either side of a baseball field's bench area. An officer then shot an arrow to put it to sleep. Then officers waited for the drug to take effect. After it fell asleep, they loaded the cow into a horse trailer. It was not clear where the cow came from or how it got lost. Police turned it over to the Animal Control Department after they caught it.`},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-ls-2`,label:`News Report 2`,questionNo:3,text:`Starting April 28th of this year, the National Museum of Natural History will begin renovating its Fossil Hall. The Fossil Hall, which displays some of the world's oldest and largest fossil specimens, receives more than two million visitors each year. It's one of the museum's most famous attractions. As a result, the museum plans to expand the hall as well as add to its ancient birds collection. Bird lovers, both young and old, have already responded with excitement at the news. The museum's social media account has been flooded with messages of support. In the meantime, the current collection will be closed. However, visitors will be compensated during the closure. The museum's special exhibition area will now be free of charge. This week, the resident exhibition is a display of ancient wall paintings on loan from Australia. They celebrate the cultural heritage of the country and will be available to view until Sunday. Next week, the exhibition will be taken over by the Wildlife Photographer of the Year competition. The winner of this year's competition will be awarded a preview of the new Fossil Hall as well as a cash prize.`},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-ls-3`,label:`News Report 3`,questionNo:5,text:`Six birds have just been trained to pick up rubbish at a French historical theme park. According to the park's manager, Mr. Villiers, the goal is not just to clear up the park. He says visitors are already good at keeping things clean. Instead, he wants to show that nature itself can teach us to take care of the environment. He says that rooks, the chosen birds, are considered to be particularly intelligent. In the right circumstances, they even like to communicate with humans and establish a relationship through play. The birds will be encouraged to clean the park through the use of a small box that delivers a small amount of bird food each time the rook deposits a cigarette end or small piece of rubbish. So far, visitors to the theme park have been excited to see the birds in action. However, some parents are concerned that it encourages their children to drop litter so they can watch the birds pick it up. Villiers is not concerned about this criticism. He maintains most of the feedback he has received has been overwhelmingly positive. He hopes now to train more birds.`},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-ls-4`,label:`Conversation 1`,questionNo:8,text:`The name of the TV show we wish to produce is Science Nation. Please tell us more. What will Science Nation be about? It'll be about science. All sorts of science. Each episode will focus on a different area of science and tell us what we know, how we know it and what we still don't know. The show will have one host only and this will be Professor Susan Powell from Harvard University. She's a great public speaker. So just to be clear, will the show's format be like that of a documentary? Kind of. It'll be like a documentary in the sense that it'll be non-fiction and fact-based. However, our idea is for it to be also fun and entertaining. Something which traditional documentaries aren't so much. Please keep in mind this will be a new TV show like nothing ever done before. OK. So it'll be both educational and entertaining. And your audience will be anyone interested in science, right? That's correct. Yes. Right. Thank you. So I think we're more or less clear on what the show will be like. Could you please tell us now what exactly you want from us? Yes, of course. Basically, what we need from you is financial support. In order to go ahead with this idea, we need two million dollars. This would cover the cost of making all 12 shows in the first season for the first year. If the show is a success, we can then look at making a second season for the following year.`},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-ls-5`,label:`Conversation 2`,questionNo:12,text:`What's up with you? You don't look very happy. I feel like I'm a failure. I can't seem to do anything very well. I wouldn't say that. You do very well in a lot of things. That presentation you gave last week was excellent. Yes, but I have this urge to strive for perfection. I really want to push harder and progress further. Well, that's very admirable, but be careful. Over-concern with being perfect can damage our confidence if we never achieve it. Yes, I know. I feel awful whenever I make a mistake in whatever I'm trying to do. Well, think about it. You can't make progress without making mistakes and learning from them. Thomas Edison, the famous inventor, once said, I've not failed. I've just found 10,000 ways that won't work. You may well be right. I guess I should recognize my mistakes and learn the lesson they teach me and move forward. Also, remember a successful ending is not the only thing worthy of a celebration. You need to recognize each step of progress you take towards achieving your goals, and no matter how tiny it is, it's still good news. I always feel down when I see others accomplishing things, and I feel miserable about my own achievements. I'm always trying to be as good as others, but I never seem to get there. Listen, if you always compare yourself with others, you'll never feel good enough. You're the only person you should be comparing yourself with. When you compare your current status with the starting point, you'll find you've made progress, right? That's good enough. That's great advice. Thank you. I'm feeling better already.`},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-ls-6`,label:`Passage 1`,questionNo:16,text:`Single-sex education can have enormous benefits for female students. Numerous studies have shown that women who attend single-sex schools tend to have stronger self-confidence, better study habits, and more ambitious career goals than women who attend coeducational schools. Girls who graduate from single-sex schools are three times more likely to become engineers than those who attend coeducational schools. The reason is that all girls' schools encourage women to enter fields traditionally dominated by men, such as science, technology, and engineering. In coeducational schools, girls are often expected to succeed only in the humanities or the arts. Research has also shown that in coeducational settings, teachers are more likely to praise and give in-depth responses to a boy's comments in class. In contrast, they might only respond to a girl's comments with a nod. They're also more likely to encourage boys to work through problems on their own, while they tend to step in and help girls who struggle with a problem. In an all-girls setting, girls are more likely to speak up frequently and make significant contributions to class than in a coeducational setting. Girls studying in a single-sex setting also earn higher scores on their college board and advanced placement exams than girls who study in coeducational settings. All girls' schools tend to be smaller than coeducational schools, which means teachers will be able to tailor the materials to girls' students' personal learning styles and interests.`},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-ls-7`,label:`Passage 2`,questionNo:19,text:`Today, I found out that Seattle doesn't really get that much rain compared with most U.S. cities. In fact, Seattle ranks 44th among major U.S. cities in average annual rainfall. Cities that get more rainfall than Seattle include Houston, Memphis, Nashville, and pretty much every major city on the eastern coast, such as New York, Boston, and Miami. So, why does everyone think of Seattle as a rainy city? The primary root of this misconception lies in that Seattle has a relatively large number of days per year with rainfall compared with New York and Boston, which get an average of about 16 percent more rain per year than Seattle, but also average between them about 36 fewer days a year of rainfall. So, it rains a lot less than Seattle, and the rain is spread out over more days than those cities. This is why few locals in Seattle carry an umbrella generally. When it does rain, it tends to be a very light rain that isn't troublesome. It almost never really rains, as most people think. On top of that, it never really storms in Seattle either. Seattle gets an average of a mere seven days a year with thunder. So, in short, if you like sunny but not too hot summers, mild winters, but with lots of cloudy days, Seattle's the place to be. Anyway, if you visit Seattle, don't bring an umbrella. People will look at you thinking you're funny.`},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-ls-8`,label:`Passage 3`,questionNo:22,text:`After a tough workout or a day full of physical activity, it's common to find your muscles aching. But where do these pains come from? According to a German professor, the soreness comes from straining your muscles in an uncommon way. For example, jumping on a bicycle for a ride. Because you haven't ridden in a long time, soreness occurs since your leg muscles aren't used to that movement. When muscles perform an activity they aren't regularly exposed to, the tiny fibers that are inside them are being torn apart. As muscle soreness develops, the body has to work to repair the muscle tears. But this doesn't happen immediately. First, the body must realize the muscles are damaged. When the body realizes the muscles are hurt, the response is to increase blood flow to the area and increase body heat. Damaged cells are then cleaned up and the body sends cells specially designed to break down the large muscle fiber fragments. Healing can take place after this. It takes about a day until these cells make it to your aching muscles. That's why there's most often a delay associated with muscle soreness. Repair of damaged cells takes about two days and afterwards the soreness disappears. Unfortunately, there's little that can be done to relieve muscle soreness. Pain relieving creams don't work, but a hot shower or warm bath can provide some relief.`}]},questions:[{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-l1`,number:1,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-ls-1`,stem:`What happened in New York's Prospect Park on Tuesday?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Many facilities were destroyed by a wandering cow.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) A wandering cow knocked down one of its fences.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Some tourists were injured by a wandering cow.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) A wandering cow was captured by the police.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`原文开头 "New York City Police captured a cow on the loose in Prospect Park on Tuesday"——周二纽约警方在展望公园捕获一头游荡的牛，选 D；文中 knocked down a police officer 是撞倒警察而非撞倒围栏，B 错。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-ls-1`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-l2`,number:2,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-ls-1`,stem:`What do we learn about the cow from the end of the news report?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It was shot to death by a police officer.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It found its way back to the park's zoo.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It became a great attraction for tourists.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It was sent to the animal control department.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`原文末句 "Police turned it over to the Animal Control Department after they caught it"——警方捕获后把牛移交给动物管理部门，选 D；射箭只是用麻醉箭使其睡着（put it to sleep）而非击毙，A 错；成为游客景点是捕获前的情况，C 错。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-ls-1`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-l3`,number:3,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-ls-2`,stem:`What does the news report say about the Fossil Hall of the National Museum of Natural History?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It is the largest of its kind.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It is going to be expanded.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It is displaying more fossil specimens.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It is starting an online exhibition.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`原文 "the museum plans to expand the hall as well as add to its ancient birds collection"——博物馆计划扩建化石大厅并增加古代鸟类收藏，选 B；文中称其藏品为世界上 oldest and largest 之一，但没说它是同类最大，A 错。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-ls-2`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-l4`,number:4,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-ls-2`,stem:`What is on display this week in the museum's resident exhibition hall?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) A collection of bird fossils from Australia.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Photographs of certain rare fossil exhibits.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Some ancient wall paintings from Australia.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Pictures by winners of a wildlife photo contest.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`原文 "This week, the resident exhibition is a display of ancient wall paintings on loan from Australia"——本周常驻展厅展出从澳大利亚借来的古代壁画，选 C；野生动物摄影大赛是下周的展览，D 错。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-ls-2`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-l5`,number:5,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-ls-3`,stem:`What have six birds been trained to do at a French historical theme park?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Pick up trash.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Amuse visitors.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Deliver messages.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Play with children.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`原文开头 "Six birds have just been trained to pick up rubbish at a French historical theme park"——六只鸟被训练在法国历史主题公园捡垃圾，pick up rubbish 即 pick up trash，选 A。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-ls-3`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-l6`,number:6,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-ls-3`,stem:`Why were rooks chosen by the park manager?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They are especially intelligent.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) They are children's favorite.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They are quite easy to tame.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They are clean and pretty.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`原文 "rooks, the chosen birds, are considered to be particularly intelligent"——被选中的秃鼻乌鸦被认为特别聪明，particularly intelligent 对应 especially intelligent，选 A。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-ls-3`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-l7`,number:7,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-ls-3`,stem:`What is the concern of some parents?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Children may be harmed by the rooks.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Children may be tempted to drop litter.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Children may contract bird diseases.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Children may overfeed the rooks.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`原文 "some parents are concerned that it encourages their children to drop litter so they can watch the birds pick it up"——家长担心这会诱使孩子扔垃圾以便看鸟捡拾，选 B。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-ls-3`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-l8`,number:8,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-ls-4`,stem:`What do we learn about the TV show Science Nation?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It will be produced at Harvard University.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It will be hosted by famous professors.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It will cover different areas of science.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It will focus on recent scientific discoveries.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`原文 "Each episode will focus on a different area of science"——每集聚焦科学的一个不同领域，即涵盖科学的多个领域，选 C；主持人是哈佛大学的 Powell 教授，但节目并非在哈佛制作，A 错；只有一位主持人，B 错。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-ls-4`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-l9`,number:9,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-ls-4`,stem:`In what way will the TV show Science Nation differ from traditional documentaries?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It will be more futuristic.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It will be more systematic.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It will be more entertaining.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It will be easier to understand.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`原文 "our idea is for it to be also fun and entertaining. Something which traditional documentaries aren't so much"——节目兼具趣味与娱乐性，这正是传统纪录片所欠缺的，选 C。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-ls-4`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-l10`,number:10,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-ls-4`,stem:`Who will be the audience of the TV show Science Nation?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) People interested in science.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Youngsters eager to explore.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Children in their early teens.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Students majoring in science.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`原文 "your audience will be anyone interested in science, right? That's correct"——观众是任何对科学感兴趣的人，选 A；并未限定为青少年或理工科学生，B、C、D 错。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-ls-4`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-l11`,number:11,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-ls-4`,stem:`What does the woman want the man to do for the TV show?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Offer professional advice.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Provide financial support.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Help promote it on the Internet.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Make episodes for its first season.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`原文 "what we need from you is financial support... we need two million dollars"——女方需要男方提供资金支持（两百万美元）以制作第一季 12 集，选 B。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-ls-4`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-l12`,number:12,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-ls-5`,stem:`How does the man feel about himself?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Unsure`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Helpless`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Concerned`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Dissatisfied`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`男士说 "I feel like I'm a failure. I can't seem to do anything very well"——他觉得自己很失败、什么都做不好，即对自己不满意，选 D。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-ls-5`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-l13`,number:13,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-ls-5`,stem:`What does the woman think is the man's problem?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) He is too concerned with being perfect.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) He loses heart when faced with setbacks.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) He is too ambitious in achieving goals.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) He takes on projects beyond his ability.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`男士自述 "I have this urge to strive for perfection"，女士回应 "Over-concern with being perfect can damage our confidence"——女士认为他的问题正是过于追求完美，选 A。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-ls-5`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-l14`,number:14,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-ls-5`,stem:`How does the man feel when he sees others accomplishing things?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Embarrassed`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Unconcerned`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Miserable`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Resentful`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`原文 "I always feel down when I see others accomplishing things, and I feel miserable about my own achievements"——看到别人取得成就时他总是情绪低落、对自己的成就感到痛苦，miserable 原词复现，选 C。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-ls-5`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-l15`,number:15,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-ls-5`,stem:`What does the woman suggest the man do?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Try to be optimistic whatever happens.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Compare his present with his past only.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Always learn from others' achievements.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Treat others the way he would be treated.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`原文 "You're the only person you should be comparing yourself with. When you compare your current status with the starting point, you'll find you've made progress"——女士建议他只跟自己比、把现状与起点比，选 B。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-ls-5`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-l16`,number:16,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-ls-6`,stem:`What advantage does the speaker say girls from single-sex schools have over those from coeducational schools?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They have a stronger sense of social responsibility.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) They are more likely to succeed in the humanities.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They are more likely to become engineers.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They have greater potential to be leaders.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`原文 "Girls who graduate from single-sex schools are three times more likely to become engineers than those who attend coeducational schools"——女校毕业生成为工程师的可能性是男女同校者的三倍，选 C；B 恰与原文"同校女生被期望只在人文艺术领域成功"相反。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-ls-6`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-l17`,number:17,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-ls-6`,stem:`What do teachers tend to do in coeducational settings?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Praise girls who like to speak up frequently.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Encourage girls to solve problems on their own.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Insist that boys and girls work together more.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Respond more positively to boys' comments.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`原文 "in coeducational settings, teachers are more likely to praise and give in-depth responses to a boy's comments in class. In contrast, they might only respond to a girl's comments with a nod"——同校环境中老师更积极回应男孩的发言，选 D；鼓励学生独立解题的也是男孩，B 错。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-ls-6`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-l18`,number:18,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-ls-6`,stem:`What are teachers more likely to do in an all-girls school?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Offer personalized teaching materials.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Provide a variety of optional courses.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Place great emphasis on test scores.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Pay extra attention to top students.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`原文末句 "teachers will be able to tailor the materials to girls' students' personal learning styles and interests"——女校老师能根据学生个人学习风格与兴趣量身定制教材，即提供个性化教学材料，选 A。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-ls-6`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-l19`,number:19,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-ls-7`,stem:`What did the speaker find out about Seattle?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It often rains cats and dogs.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It seldom rains in summer time.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It does not rain as much as people think.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It is one of the most rainy cities in the US.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`原文开头 "Seattle doesn't really get that much rain compared with most U.S. cities... ranks 44th among major U.S. cities in average annual rainfall"——西雅图的降雨量并没有人们以为的那么多，选 C；D 与原文正好相反。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-ls-7`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-l20`,number:20,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-ls-7`,stem:`Why do local people in Seattle seldom carry an umbrella?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They drive most of the time.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) The rain is usually very light.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They have got used to the rain.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) The rain comes mostly at night.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`原文 "When it does rain, it tends to be a very light rain that isn't troublesome"——即使下雨也往往是很小的雨、不碍事，所以当地人一般不带伞，选 B。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-ls-7`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-l21`,number:21,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-ls-7`,stem:`Why does the speaker say Seattle is a good place to be?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It has a lot of places for entertainment.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It has never seen thunder and lightning.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It has fewer cloudy days than any other coastal city.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It has mild weather both in summer and in winter.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`原文 "if you like sunny but not too hot summers, mild winters, but with lots of cloudy days, Seattle's the place to be"——西雅图夏天晴朗但不太热、冬天温和，故冬夏气候宜人，选 D；文中说每年约有 7 天打雷，B 的 never 过于绝对；多云天很多，C 错。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-ls-7`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-l22`,number:22,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-ls-8`,stem:`What does the German professor say about muscle soreness?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It occurs when people are doing a repetitive activity.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It results from exerting one's muscles continuously.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It happens when people engage in an uncommon activity.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It comes from straining one's muscles in an unusual way.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`原文 "the soreness comes from straining your muscles in an uncommon way"——酸痛源于以不寻常的方式拉伤肌肉，unusual way 即 uncommon way，选 D；C 只说"参与不常见的活动"，未点明"拉伤肌肉"，不如 D 准确。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-ls-8`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-l23`,number:23,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-ls-8`,stem:`What happens when muscles are damaged according to the passage?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Blood flow and body heat increase in the affected area.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Body movements in the affected area become difficult.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They begin to make repairs immediately.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They gradually become fragmented.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`原文 "When the body realizes the muscles are hurt, the response is to increase blood flow to the area and increase body heat"——肌肉受损时身体反应是增加该部位血流并提高体温，选 A；原文 "this doesn't happen immediately" 否定 C。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-ls-8`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-l24`,number:24,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-ls-8`,stem:`How long does it take for damaged cells to heal?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) About one week.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) About two days.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) About ten days.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) About four weeks.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`原文 "Repair of damaged cells takes about two days and afterwards the soreness disappears"——受损细胞修复约需两天，选 B；"It takes about a day until these cells make it to your aching muscles" 说的是细胞到达患处需一天，属干扰项。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-ls-8`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-l25`,number:25,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-ls-8`,stem:`What does the speaker suggest one do to relieve muscle soreness?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Apply muscle creams.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Drink plenty of water.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Have a hot shower.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Take pain-killers.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`原文末句 "Pain relieving creams don't work, but a hot shower or warm bath can provide some relief"——止痛膏无效，但洗热水澡或泡温水浴可缓解酸痛，选 C。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-ls-8`},score:1}]},{id:`reading`,kind:`reading`,title:`Part III 阅读理解`,instructions:`Directions: In this section, there is a passage with ten blanks. You are required to select one word for each blank from a list of choices given in a word bank following the passage. Read the passage through carefully before making your choices. Each choice in the bank is identified by a letter. Please mark the corresponding letter for each item on Answer Sheet 2 with a single line through the centre. You may not use any of the words in the bank more than once. Directions: In this section, you are going to read a passage with ten statements attached to it. Each statement contains information given in one of the paragraphs. Identify the paragraph from which the information is derived. You may choose a paragraph more than once. Each paragraph is marked with a letter. Answer the questions by marking the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 2. Directions: There are 2 passages in this section. Each passage is followed by some questions or unfinished statements. For each of them there are four choices marked A), B), C) and D). You should decide on the best choice and mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 2 with a single line through the centre.`,minutes:40,bank:{A:`adjusted`,B:`channels`,C:`equivalent`,D:`experience`,E:`filters`,F:`frequently`,G:`individuals`,H:`originally`,I:`particular`,J:`primarily`,K:`reduce`,L:`renovated`,M:`smooth`,N:`thrive`,O:`unpleasant`},passages:[{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-cp`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-cp-1`,text:`Millions of people travel by plane every single day. If you're planning on being one of them soon, you might not be looking forward to the __26__ feeling air travel often leaves you with. Besides the airport crowds and stress, travelling at a high altitude has real effects on the body. Although the pressure of the cabin is __27__ to prevent altitude sickness, you could still __28__ sleepless or a headache. The lower oxygen pressure found in an aircraft cabin is __29__ to that at 6,000-8,000 feet of altitude. A drop in oxygen pressure can cause headaches in certain __30__. To help prevent headaches, drink plenty of water, and avoid alcohol and coffee.`},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-cp-2`,text:`Airplane food might not really be as tasteless as you __31__ thought. The air you breathe in a plane dries out your mouth and nose, which can affect your sense of taste. Perception of sweet and salty foods dropped by almost 30 percent in a simulation of air travel. However, you can make your taste buds active again by drinking water. A dry mouth may __32__ taste sensitivity, but taste is restored by drinking fluids.`},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-cp-3`,text:`Although in-flight infections __33__ in dry environments like airplanes, your risk of getting sick from an airplane is actually low because of the air __34__ used. Unless you're sitting next to someone who is coughing or sneezing, you shouldn't worry too much about getting sick. However, bacteria have been shown to live on cabin surfaces, so wash your hands __35__.`}]},t,{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-p1`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-p1-1`,text:`The fifth-largest city in the US passed a significant soda tax proposal that will levy (征税) 1.5 cents per liquid ounce on distributors. Philadelphia's new measure was approved by a 13 to 4 city council vote. It sets a new bar for similar initiatives across the country. It is proof that taxes on sugary drinks can gain substantial support outside super-liberal areas. Until now, the only city to successfully pass and implement a soda tax was Berkeley, California, in 2014.`},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-p1-2`,text:`The tax will apply to regular and diet sodas, as well as other drinks with added sugar, such as Gatorade and iced teas. It's expected to raise $410 million over the next five years, most of which will go toward funding a universal pre-kindergarten program for the city.`},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-p1-3`,text:`While the city council vote was met with applause inside the council room, opponents to the measure, including soda lobbyists, made sharp criticisms and a promise to challenge the tax in court.`},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-p1-4`,text:`"The tax passed today unfairly singles out beverages—including low- and no-calorie choices," said Lauren Kane, spokeswoman for the American Beverage Association. "But most importantly, it is against the law. So we will side with the majority of the people of Philadelphia who oppose this tax and take legal action to stop it."`},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-p1-5`,text:`An industry-backed anti-tax campaign has spent at least $4 million on advertisements. The ads criticized the measure, characterizing it as a "grocery tax."`},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-p1-6`,text:`Public health groups applauded the approved tax as a step toward fixing certain lasting health issues that plague Americans. "The move to recapture a small part of the profits from an industry that pushes a product that contributes to diabetes, obesity, and heart disease in poorer communities in order to reinvest in those communities will surely be inspirational to many other places," said Jim Krieger, executive director of Healthy Food America. "Indeed, we are already hearing from some of them. It's not 'just Berkeley' anymore."`},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-p1-7`,text:`Similar measures in California's Albany, Oakland, San Francisco, and Colorado's Boulder are becoming hot-button issues. Health advocacy groups have hinted that even more might be coming.`}]},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-p2`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-p2-1`,text:`Popping food into the microwave for a couple of minutes may seem utterly harmless, but Europe's stock of these quick-cooking ovens emits as much carbon as nearly 7 million cars, a new study has found. And the problem is growing. With costs falling and kitchen appliances becoming "status" items, owners are throwing away microwaves after an average of eight years. This is putting sales of new microwaves, which are expected to reach 13.5 million annually in the EU by the end of the decade.`},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-p2-2`,text:`A study by the University of Manchester calculated the emissions of CO2—the main greenhouse gas responsible for climate change—at every stage of microwaves, from manufacture to waste disposal. "It is electricity consumption by microwaves that has the biggest impact on the environment," say the authors. The authors also calculate that the emissions from using 19 microwaves over a year are the same as those from using a car. According to the same study, efforts to reduce consumption should focus on improving consumer awareness and behavior. For example, consumers could use appliances in a more efficient way by adjusting the time of cooking to the type of food.`},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-p2-3`,text:`However, David Reay, professor of carbon management, argues that, although microwaves use a great deal of energy, their emissions are minor compared to those from cars. In the UK alone, there are around 30 million cars. These cars emit more than all the microwaves in the EU. Backing this up, recent data show that passenger cars in the UK emitted 69 million tons of CO2 in 2015. This is 10 times the amount this new microwave oven study estimates for annual emissions for all the microwave ovens in the EU. Further, the energy used by microwaves is lower than any other form of cooking. Among common kitchen appliances used for cooking, microwaves are the most energy efficient, followed by a stove and finally a standard oven. Thus, rising microwave sales could be seen as a positive thing.`}]}],questions:[{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-c26`,number:26,type:`bank`,stem:`you might not be looking forward to the __26__ feeling air travel often leaves you with.`,answer:`O`,explanation:`unpleasant 不愉快的：定冠词 the 与名词 feeling 之间需形容词，指"航空旅行常给人留下的不愉快感受"，与下文机场拥挤、压力及高空对身体的影响相呼应，选 O。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-cp-1`,quote:`the __26__ feeling air travel often leaves you with.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-c27`,number:27,type:`bank`,stem:`Although the pressure of the cabin is __27__ to prevent altitude sickness...`,answer:`A`,explanation:`adjusted 调节过的：is 后需过去分词构成被动，the pressure of the cabin is adjusted 指"机舱压力被调节"以防止高原反应，符合句意，选 A。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-cp-1`,quote:`the pressure of the cabin is __27__ to prevent altitude sickness`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-c28`,number:28,type:`bank`,stem:`...you could still __28__ sleepless or a headache.`,answer:`D`,explanation:`experience 经历、感受到：情态动词 could 后需动词原形，experience sleepless or a headache 指"仍会感到失眠或头痛"，选 D。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-cp-1`,quote:`you could still __28__ sleepless or a headache.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-c29`,number:29,type:`bank`,stem:`The lower oxygen pressure found in an aircraft cabin is __29__ to that at 6,000-8,000 feet of altitude.`,answer:`C`,explanation:`equivalent 等同的：be equivalent to 为固定搭配，指"机舱内较低的气压相当于海拔 6000–8000 英尺处的气压"，选 C。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-cp-1`,quote:`is __29__ to that at 6,000-8,000 feet of altitude.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-c30`,number:30,type:`bank`,stem:`A drop in oxygen pressure can cause headaches in certain __30__.`,answer:`G`,explanation:`individuals 个体、人：certain 后需可数名词复数，in certain individuals 指"在某些人身上"，即气压下降会使某些人头痛，选 G。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-cp-1`,quote:`cause headaches in certain __30__.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-c31`,number:31,type:`bank`,stem:`Airplane food might not really be as tasteless as you __31__ thought.`,answer:`H`,explanation:`originally 最初、原本：修饰动词 thought 需副词，as you originally thought 指"不像你原本想的那样寡淡"，选 H。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-cp-2`,quote:`as tasteless as you __31__ thought.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-c32`,number:32,type:`bank`,stem:`A dry mouth may __32__ taste sensitivity, but taste is restored by drinking fluids.`,answer:`K`,explanation:`reduce 降低：情态动词 may 后需动词原形，与后半句 but taste is restored（味觉恢复）形成对比，指"口干会降低味觉敏感度"，选 K。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-cp-2`,quote:`A dry mouth may __32__ taste sensitivity`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-c33`,number:33,type:`bank`,stem:`Although in-flight infections __33__ in dry environments like airplanes...`,answer:`N`,explanation:`thrive 滋生、旺盛生长：主语 infections 为复数，需动词原形，指"机内感染在像飞机这样的干燥环境中滋生"，与后半句"患病风险其实很低"构成让步转折，选 N。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-cp-3`,quote:`in-flight infections __33__ in dry environments like airplanes`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-c34`,number:34,type:`bank`,stem:`...your risk of getting sick from an airplane is actually low because of the air __34__ used.`,answer:`E`,explanation:`filters 过滤器：the air __ used 指"所使用的空气过滤装置"，air filters 为常见搭配，解释了患病风险低的原因，选 E。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-cp-3`,quote:`because of the air __34__ used.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-c35`,number:35,type:`bank`,stem:`However, bacteria have been shown to live on cabin surfaces, so wash your hands __35__.`,answer:`F`,explanation:`frequently 频繁地：修饰动词 wash 需副词，指"细菌会在机舱表面存活，所以要勤洗手"，选 F。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-cp-3`,quote:`so wash your hands __35__.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-m36`,number:36,type:`single`,stem:`Songdo's popularity lies more in its quality of life than its business attraction.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`F`,explanation:`F 段 "Songdo has become a popular Korean city—more popular as a residential area than a business one" 且 "It's a great place to live"——松岛作为居住地比商业中心更受欢迎，其人气更多来自生活质量，选 F。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-mp-F`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-m37`,number:37,type:`single`,stem:`The man who conceived Songdo feels disappointed because it has fallen short of his expectations.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`I`,explanation:`I 段 "Park Yeon Soo, the man who first imagined Songdo, feels frustrated, too... we're waiting for a good driver to accelerate"——构想者 Park 感到失望沮丧，因为城市尚未达到他期望的"好车需要好司机来加速"的状态，选 I。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-mp-I`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-m38`,number:38,type:`single`,stem:`A scene in a popular South Korean music video was shot in Songdo.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`D`,explanation:`D 段 "'Gangnam Style'... some of the video was filmed in Songdo... there was a scene in a subway station. That was actually Songdo"——热门韩语 MV《江南 Style》的部分场景在松岛拍摄，选 D。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-mp-D`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-m39`,number:39,type:`single`,stem:`Songdo still lacks the financial stimulus for businesses to set up shop there.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`G`,explanation:`G 段 "there needs to be strong economic incentives for companies to locate here"——仍需要强有力的经济激励来吸引企业入驻，即缺乏商业刺激，选 G。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-mp-G`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-m40`,number:40,type:`single`,stem:`Airplanes will increasingly become the chief means of transportation, according to a professor.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`B`,explanation:`B 段 Kasarda 教授说 "the 21st century will increasingly be an aviation century, as the globe becomes increasingly connected by air"——21 世纪将日益成为航空世纪，飞机将成为主要交通方式，选 B。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-mp-B`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-m41`,number:41,type:`single`,stem:`Songdo has ended up different from the city it was supposed to be.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`E`,explanation:`E 段 "The city was supposed to be a hub for global companies... But that's not how it has turned out. Songdo's reputation is as a futuristic ghost town"——松岛本应是全球企业枢纽，结果却成了"未来鬼城"，与初衷不同，选 E。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-mp-E`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-m42`,number:42,type:`single`,stem:`Some of the people who work in Songdo complain about boredom in the workplace.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`H`,explanation:`H 段 "to some of the residents, Songdo feels hollow. 'I'm, like, in prison for weekdays. That's what we call it in the workplace'"——有上班族把工作日比作"坐牢"，抱怨工作场所枯燥空洞，选 H。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-mp-H`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-m43`,number:43,type:`single`,stem:`A business professor says that a future city should have easy access to international transportation.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`A`,explanation:`A 段 "a University of North Carolina business professor named John Kasarda... future cities should be built intentionally around or near airports... offer businesses 'rapid, long-distance connectivity' "——商科教授认为未来城市应围绕机场而建、便于航空连通，选 A。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-mp-A`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-m44`,number:44,type:`single`,stem:`According to an urban design professor, it is difficult for city designers to foresee what will happen in the future.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`J`,explanation:`J 段 "Brent Ryan, who teaches urban design at MIT... when it comes to cities—or anything else—it is hard to predict the future"——城市规划教授认为未来难以预测，设计者难以预见将来，选 J。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-mp-J`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-m45`,number:45,type:`single`,stem:`Park Yeon Soo, who envisioned Songdo, feels a parental connection with the city.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`C`,explanation:`C 段 "Park Yeon Soo conceived this city of the future back in 1986. He considers Songdo his baby"——构想者 Park 把松岛当作自己的孩子，体现出父母般的情感联结，选 C。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-mp-C`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-r46`,number:46,type:`single`,stem:`What does the passage say about the newly-approved soda tax in Philadelphia?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It will change the lifestyle of many consumers.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It may encourage other US cities to follow suit.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It will cut soda consumption among low-income communities.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It may influence the marketing strategies of the soda business.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`第一段 "It sets a new bar for similar initiatives across the country... It's not 'just Berkeley' anymore"——费城汽水税为全国类似举措树立新标杆，可能鼓励其他美国城市效仿，选 B。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-p1-1`,quote:`It sets a new bar for similar initiatives across the country.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-r47`,number:47,type:`single`,stem:`What will the opponents probably do to respond to the soda tax proposal?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Bargain with the city council.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Refuse to pay additional tax.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Take legal action against it.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Try to gain public support.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`第三、四段 "a promise to challenge the tax in court... take legal action to stop it"——反对者扬言在法庭上挑战该税并采取法律行动阻止它，选 C。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-p1-4`,quote:`take legal action to stop it.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-r48`,number:48,type:`single`,stem:`What did the industry-backed anti-tax campaign do about the soda tax proposal?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It tried to arouse hostile feelings among consumers.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It tried to win grocers' support against the measure.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It kept sending letters of protest to the media.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It criticized the measure through advertising.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`第五段 "An industry-backed anti-tax campaign has spent at least $4 million on advertisements. The ads criticized the measure"——行业支持的反税运动花费至少 400 万美元做广告批评该措施，选 D。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-p1-5`,quote:`The ads criticized the measure, characterizing it as a "grocery tax."`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-r49`,number:49,type:`single`,stem:`What did public health groups think the soda tax would do?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Alert people to the risk of sugar-induced diseases.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Help people to fix certain long-time health issues.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Add to the fund for their research on diseases.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Benefit low-income people across the country.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`第六段 "Public health groups applauded the approved tax as a step toward fixing certain lasting health issues that plague Americans"——公共卫生团体称赞该税是解决困扰美国人的长期健康问题的一步，选 B。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-p1-6`,quote:`a step toward fixing certain lasting health issues that plague Americans`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-r50`,number:50,type:`single`,stem:`What do we learn about similar measures concerning the soda tax in some other cities?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They are becoming rather sensitive issues.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) They are spreading panic in the soda industry.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They are reducing the incidence of sugar-induced diseases.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They are taking away a lot of profit from the soda industry.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`末段 "Similar measures in California's Albany, Oakland, San Francisco, and Colorado's Boulder are becoming hot-button issues"——其他城市的类似举措正成为热点/敏感议题，hot-button 即 sensitive，选 A。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-p1-7`,quote:`are becoming hot-button issues.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-r51`,number:51,type:`single`,stem:`What is the finding of the new study?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Quick-cooking microwave ovens have become more popular.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) The frequent use of microwaves may do harm to our health.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) CO2 emissions constitute a major threat to the environment.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) The use of microwaves emits more CO2 than people think.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`第一段 "Europe's stock of these quick-cooking ovens emits as much carbon as nearly 7 million cars, a new study has found"——新研究发现欧洲微波炉的碳排放相当于近 700 万辆汽车，即微波炉使用排放的二氧化碳比人们想的多，选 D。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-p2-1`,quote:`emits as much carbon as nearly 7 million cars, a new study has found.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-r52`,number:52,type:`single`,stem:`Why are the sales of microwaves expected to rise?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They are becoming more affordable.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) They have a shorter life cycle than other appliances.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They are getting much easier to operate.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They take less time to cook than other appliances.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`第一段 "With costs falling and kitchen appliances becoming 'status' items, owners are throwing away microwaves after an average of eight years"——成本下降使微波炉更便宜、更买得起，且平均八年就被丢弃，推动了新机销量，选 A。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-p2-1`,quote:`With costs falling and kitchen appliances becoming "status" items`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-r53`,number:53,type:`single`,stem:`What recommendation does the study by the University of Manchester make?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Cooking food of different varieties.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Improving microwave users' habits.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Eating less to cut energy consumption.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Using microwave ovens less frequently.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`第二段 "efforts to reduce consumption should focus on improving consumer awareness and behavior. For example, consumers could use appliances in a more efficient way by adjusting the time of cooking"——研究建议着力改善消费者的意识与行为（如按食物种类调整烹饪时间），即改善微波炉使用者的习惯，选 B。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-p2-2`,quote:`efforts to reduce consumption should focus on improving consumer awareness and behavior.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-r54`,number:54,type:`single`,stem:`What does Professor David Reay try to argue?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) There are far more emissions from cars than from microwaves.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) People should be persuaded to use passenger cars less often.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) The UK produces less CO2 than many other countries in the EU.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) More data are needed to show whether microwaves are harmful.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`第三段 "their emissions are minor compared to those from cars... These cars emit more than all the microwaves in the EU"——David Reay 教授认为与汽车相比微波炉排放很小，汽车排放远多于微波炉，选 A。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-p2-3`,quote:`These cars emit more than all the microwaves in the EU.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-r55`,number:55,type:`single`,stem:`What does Professor David Reay think of the use of microwaves?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It will become less popular in the coming decades.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It makes everyday cooking much more convenient.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It plays a positive role in environmental protection.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It consumes more power than conventional cooking.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`末段 "microwaves are the most energy efficient... Thus, rising microwave sales could be seen as a positive thing"——微波炉是最节能的烹饪方式，销量上升可视为好事，即其对环保起积极作用，选 C；D 与原文"能耗低于其他烹饪方式"相悖。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2019-12-s3-p2-3`,quote:`Thus, rising microwave sales could be seen as a positive thing.`},score:1}]},{id:`translation`,kind:`translation`,title:`Part IV 翻译`,instructions:`Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to translate a passage from Chinese into English. You should write your answer on Answer Sheet 2.`,minutes:30,reference:{translation:`The full name of Han Chinese people consists of a surname and a given name. A characteristic of Chinese names is that the surname always comes first, followed by the given name. For thousands of years, the paternal surname has been passed down through generations. However, nowadays, it is not uncommon for children to take their mother's surname. Generally, the given name consists of one or two Chinese characters, often carrying the parents' wishes for their child. From a child's name, one can infer what kind of person the parents hope their child will become or what kind of life they expect them to lead. Parents place great importance on naming their children, as the name often accompanies the child for life.`},questions:[{id:`cet4-2019-12-s3-t1`,number:1,type:`translation`,stem:`中国汉族人的全名由姓和名组成。中文姓名的特点是，姓总是在前，名跟在其后。千百年来，父姓一直世代相传。然而，如今，孩子跟母亲姓并不罕见。一般来说，名有一个或两个汉字，通常承载父母对孩子的愿望。从孩子的名字可以推断出父母希望孩子成为什么样的人，或者期望他们过什么样的生活。父母非常重视给孩子取名，因为名字往往会伴随孩子一生。`,answer:`见参考`,explanation:`要点：①"全名由姓和名组成"译作 the full name... consists of a surname and a given name；"姓总是在前，名跟在其后"用 the surname always comes first, followed by the given name。②"父姓一直世代相传"用 the paternal surname has been passed down through generations（现在完成时 + 被动）；"并不罕见"用 it is not uncommon for children to take their mother's surname。③"承载父母对孩子的愿望"译作 often carrying the parents' wishes for their child（现在分词作伴随）；"推断出"用 one can infer what... or what...，后接宾语从句。④"非常重视取名"用 place great importance on naming their children；"伴随孩子一生"用 the name often accompanies the child for life（as 引导原因状语从句）；全文以一般现在时为主，注意长句切分与逻辑衔接。`,score:15}]}]}};export{n as default};